  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon\n\nScope check first, since it shapes everything below: `web/` is a real, working Next.js console. `engine/` is almost entirely `raise NotImplementedError` stubs (`pipeline/blocking.py`, `detect.py`, `normalize.py`, `db.py`, `agent/graph.py`, `agent/tools.py`, `agent/prompts.py`, connectors). So there are no live N+1 patterns or hot loops to fix in the engine yet — but the stub signatures already commit to specific shapes, and a couple of them are exactly where N+1 risk gets baked in if the wrong shape gets implemented. I've flagged those as design constraints, not bugs.\n\nThe web layer itself is unusually disciplined already (joined queries, `Promise.all` for independent reads, server-components-by-default). Findings are correspondingly modest — I'm not going to manufacture severity that isn't there.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns\n\n**Web layer: none found.** Every multi-table read is a single joined query:\n- `listRuns`/`getRunDetail` (`web/lib/server/runs.ts:51-79`) join `runs` ↔ `sources` (aliased twice) ↔ `rulesets` in one query rather than fetching the run then looking up names.\n- `loadDecisions` (`web/lib/server/export.ts:112-145`) joins `conflicts` → `matches` → `entities` (aliased A/B) → `resolutions` in one query for the whole decision log, rather than per-conflict lookups.\n- `findSourceIds` (`web/lib/server/sources.ts:41-50`) uses `inArray` for a batch existence check instead of one query per id.\n- Independent reads are parallelized with `Promise.all` (`runs/[runId]/page.tsx:72-75`, `rules/page.tsx:16`, `api/runs/route.ts:37-40`) rather than awaited sequentially.\n\n**Engine: latent risk in the stub contracts.** `pipeline/detect.py::detect()` takes `entities: dict[UUID, CanonicalEntity]` — good, that's a bulk-loaded lookup table, not per-match fetches. But `db.py` currently has no read function for entities/matches at all, only writes. When Phase 2 fills in `execute_run` in `main.py`, the entities dict must be built with **one query per run**, not one per match:\n\n```python\n# db.py — add, don't fetch per match\ndef load_entities(run_id: UUID) -> dict[UUID, CanonicalEntity]:\n    rows = conn.execute(\n        \"SELECT * FROM entities WHERE run_id = %s\", (run_id,)\n    ).fetchall()\n    return {row.id: CanonicalEntity(...) for row in rows}\n\n# main.py\nentities = db.load_entities(run_id)          # 1 query\nmatches = pipeline.match(...)                 # pure, in-memory\nconflicts = pipeline.detect(matches, entities)  # dict lookups, no I/O\n```\nThe pattern to avoid is calling a per-entity DB read from inside `detect()`'s diff loop — easy to introduce by accident since `detect` currently has no data access at all to constrain it.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders in React\n\n**Real, small finding — `RulesetEditor` (`web/components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166-179`):**\n\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);        // O(n) per row\n  return (\n    <RuleRow\n      key={rule.id}\n      position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}  // another O(n) per row\n      onChange={(patch) => update(index, patch)}\n      ...\n```\nTwo `indexOf` calls per row make the render O(n²), and `update`/`removeRule` allocate a new closure and a new `rules` array on every keystroke, so **every** `RuleRow` re-renders when any single field changes (no memoization anywhere in the tree). At realistic ruleset sizes (tens of rules) this is invisible, so it's not urgent — but it's a one-line-cause, cheap fix:\n\n```tsx\nconst indexById = new Map(rules.map((rule, i) => [rule.id, i]));\n\n{ordered.map((rule, position) => (\n  <RuleRow\n    key={rule.id}\n    rule={rule}\n    position={position + 1}\n    sources={sources}\n    onChange={(patch) => update(indexById.get(rule.id)!, patch)}\n    onRemove={rules.length > 1 ? () => removeRule(indexById.get(rule.id)!) : undefined}\n  />\n))}\n```\nThis drops it to O(n) and removes the `indexOf` dependency entirely.\n\n**Forward-looking, more important:** AGENTS.md commits to a **400-row conflict queue** with `j`/`k` keyboard navigation (§ Quality floor), and that page doesn't exist yet (no `app/(console)/conflicts/`). This is exactly the shape that breaks under naive state management: a single `selectedIndex` state at the list root, re-rendered on every keypress, will re-render all 400 `ConflictDiffRow`s per `j`/`k` press if rows aren't isolated. When you build it:\n\n```tsx\n// Row must not re-render just because the cursor moved past it\nconst ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({\n  conflict, selected,\n}: { conflict: Conflict; selected: boolean }) {\n  return <div className={selected ? \"ring-2 ring-g-900\" : \"\"}>...</div>;\n});\n\n// Parent passes only the primitive each row needs to compare against\n{conflicts.map((c) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow key={c.id} conflict={c} selected={c.id === selectedId} />\n))}\n```\n`React.memo` + comparing `c.id === selectedId` (not passing the whole selection state) keeps a `j`/`k` press to a 2-row re-render (old selection, new selection) regardless of queue length. Also virtualize the list past a few hundred rows — nothing in the current codebase renders that many DOM nodes, but the conflict queue is designed to.\n\n**Minor, optional — `RunLive` (`web/components/runs/run-live.tsx:46-60`):** `useQuery` polls every 2s with `staleTime: 0`, and each response is freshly parsed by Zod, so it's a new object reference every tick even when nothing changed (e.g., stuck in `\"extracting\"`). React reconciles this cheaply for a tree this small, so it's not worth a fix on its own — but if this screen grows heavier, add a `select`/equality check so identical stats don't trigger a re-render.\n\nEverything else (`RunTable`, `SourceTable`, `RunTimeline`, `SeverityHistogram`, `StatGrid`) is a Server Component or a pure presentational function with no internal state — nothing to over-render.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n**Already correct, worth naming so it isn't \"fixed\" by accident:** `web/lib/db/index.ts:22-44` caches the Postgres connection on `globalThis`, specifically to survive Next.js HMR reloads in dev without leaking pooler connections. Don't add a `cache()`-wrapped factory on top of this — it would fight the existing singleton.\n\n**Real, unimplemented cost lever — prompt caching (engine, Phase 3):** `agent/prompts.py:1-6` already documents the intent (\"the ruleset block is IDENTICAL across every call in a run... the single largest cost lever\") but `build_ruleset_block` is a stub. When implemented, the cache breakpoint has to go on the ruleset block specifically, with per-conflict content strictly after it:\n\n```python\ndef call_resolution_model(ruleset_block: str, conflict_block: str) -> Message:\n    return client.messages.create(\n        model=MODEL_RESOLUTION,\n        system=[\n            {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": ruleset_block,\n             \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},   # cached prefix\n        ],\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": conflict_block}],  # varies per call\n    )\n```\nGetting the split wrong (e.g., interpolating anything conflict-specific into `ruleset_block`) silently defeats the cache on every call — worth a unit test asserting `build_ruleset_block(rules)` is byte-identical across two different conflicts in the same run.\n\n**Minor, preventive — per-request query dedup:** Next's automatic request memoization only covers `fetch()`; direct Drizzle calls in `lib/server/*.ts` (`listSources`, `listRulesets`, `getActiveRuleset`) are not deduped if two components in the same render tree both call them. I didn't find an actual duplicate call today — `NavRail` gets identity from Clerk's `useUser()`, not from these functions — but it's one call away from happening as pages grow (e.g., a future breadcrumb that also needs the active ruleset). Wrapping the read functions in React's `cache()` is a cheap guard:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => { ... });\n```\nI'd hold off until there's an actual second call site — per AGENTS.md's own philosophy, this is exactly the kind of preemptive abstraction to skip until it's earning its keep.\n\n**Deliberately not cached, and correctly so:** `connection()` in `listSources`/`listRulesets`/`getRunDetail` opts out of static rendering on purpose (comment at `sources.ts:15-18`: \"configured sources are request-time state, not build-time\"). Don't add HTTP or fetch-level caching to these routes — staleness here means a reviewer starts a run against a deleted source.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory leaks\n\nChecked every `useEffect`/`setInterval`/`setState`-in-callback site in the client components:\n\n- `Elapsed` (`run-live.tsx:98-118`) — `setInterval` is correctly cleared in the effect's cleanup, and the effect re-runs only on `running`/`startedAt` changes. Clean.\n- `QueryProvider` (`query-provider.tsx:14-32`) — `QueryClient` is created once via `useState(() => ...)`, not recreated on every render, and the provider is mounted at the `RunLive` leaf rather than the root, so it doesn't outlive the page that needs it. Clean.\n- `RunProgress`'s `router.refresh()` effect (`run-live.tsx:66-68`) has no cleanup need — it fires once and the component unmounts on navigation.\n- No manual `addEventListener`, `IntersectionObserver`, `ResizeObserver`, or WebSocket usage anywhere in `web/` — nothing else to leak.\n\nNo findings here. The one thing to watch when Phase 2/3 land in `engine/`: if `pipeline/blocking.py::match()` loads a `sentence-transformers` model or builds a FAISS index, that has to happen **once at process start** (module-level or a lazily-initialized singleton), not per run — reloading a transformer model per HTTP request would be a real leak/latency problem and there's no guard against it yet since the function is unimplemented.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n- `RulesetEditor`'s O(n²) `indexOf` pattern above is the one concrete redundant-computation finding — same fix applies.\n- `SeverityHistogram` (`severity-histogram.tsx:46-49`) recomputes `total`/`peak` via `.reduce`/`Math.max` on every render, but over a fixed 4-element array — not worth memoizing.\n- `orderRulesByEvaluation` (`web/types/rules.ts:88-93`) does a stable sort on every render of `RulesetEditor` rather than only when `rules` changes — again, fine at real ruleset sizes (dozens, not thousands), so a `useMemo` here would be defensive rather than necessary. If you add it, key it on `rules`:\n  ```tsx\n  const ordered = useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules]);\n  ```\n- No other duplicated computation found — `export.ts`'s `applyDecisions` builds one `Map` from `decisions` and does a single pass over `accounts` (`O(n + m)`, not nested loops).\n\n---\n\n### Bottom line\n\nThe web layer doesn't need performance surgery right now — the two real findings (`RulesetEditor`'s indexOf pattern, and no memoization strategy for a conflict queue that doesn't exist yet) are cheap to fix and cheap to defer. The higher-leverage move is making sure the **engine's still-unwritten** `db.load_entities`, `blocking.match`, and `prompts.build_ruleset_block` land in the shapes sketched above the first time — those are exactly the three places (bulk entity load, O(n·k) blocking, and the prompt-cache prefix) where AGENTS.md is explicit that getting it wrong is the difference between a 500-account demo and something that survives a real org.\n",
